{
  "id": "EDU050",
  "title": "The Summary Drops Its Anchor",
  "family": "learning",
  "category": "language/communication",
  "premise": "A reporter wants to shorten a discussion, but cuts away the condition that made a proposal acceptable. A comparison restores the condition and labels remaining disagreement instead of inventing a unanimous decision.",
  "hook": "What disappeared when the meeting became one sentence?",
  "visible_action": "A long transcript folds into summary cards; a dangling condition tag signals a lost qualification.",
  "intended_takeaway_or_theme": "A concise summary should preserve material conditions, uncertainty, and disagreement.",
  "gate": "Use an invented meeting; do not attribute decisions or approval to real people.",
